WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.825
In this video, we follow camera frames through V four L two, the Linux video API, and look at the audio side with ALSA.

00:00:08.225 --> 00:00:18.326
V4L2: Video for Linux 2: the kernel interface every Linux camera and video device uses. Like: The standard loading dock every camera truck backs into.

00:00:18.626 --> 00:00:30.549
ALSA / ASoC: Advanced Linux Sound Architecture: the kernel's audio system; ASoC is its part for chips on boards. Like: The plumbing that carries sound from microphones to programs and back to speakers.

00:00:31.649 --> 00:00:35.086
The camera starts streaming, but every frame is bright green.

00:00:35.336 --> 00:00:40.200
The sensor, the image processor and the app each thought the pixels were packed a different way.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.827
Let's follow a frame from sensor to app, and a sound from microphone to speaker.

00:00:45.877 --> 00:00:49.047
V four L two works like a cafeteria tray system.

00:00:49.447 --> 00:00:52.814
You hand empty trays to the kitchen. That's queueing a buffer.

00:00:53.214 --> 00:00:57.626
The kitchen fills them with food. That's the hardware writing a frame by DMA.

00:00:58.026 --> 00:01:09.194
DMA: Direct memory access: a hardware helper that copies data to memory without the processor doing each byte. Like: A conveyor belt that moves boxes while the worker does something else.

00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:12.606
You take a full tray off the counter. That's dequeueing.

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:14.933
You eat, then hand the tray back.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:21.335
If you're slow to hand trays back, the kitchen runs out, and drops meals. Those are dropped frames.

00:01:22.535 --> 00:01:23.232
Words.

00:01:23.632 --> 00:01:32.723
V four L two is the Linux kernel API for cameras, video encoders and HDMI capture. It shows up as slash dev slash video nodes.

00:01:33.123 --> 00:01:39.845
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:01:40.095 --> 00:01:48.547
HDMI: The common cable standard for sending video and audio to TVs and monitors. Like: A garden hose for pictures and sound.

00:01:48.847 --> 00:01:57.520
The media controller describes the graph: sensor, then the CSI receiver, then the image signal processor, then the video node.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:09.924
Media controller: A kernel interface that describes and connects the blocks of a camera pipeline (sensor, image processor, outputs). Like: A train-yard map with switch levers you set before the train runs.

00:02:10.224 --> 00:02:17.910
MIPI CSI: The fast camera interface between an image sensor and the processor. Like: A dedicated pipe from the camera eye to the brain.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.937
A subdevice is one block in that graph, with its own formats.

00:02:22.337 --> 00:02:30.510
ALSA is Linux audio. Its system on chip layer, called ASoC, splits drivers into codec, platform and machine parts.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:42.752
SoC: System on a chip: one chip holding the processor cores, graphics, video, USB and more. Like: A whole city on one island: houses, factories and roads in one place.

00:02:43.052 --> 00:02:47.673
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:53.589
Codec: Software or hardware that compresses and decompresses video or audio. Like: Vacuum-packing clothes for travel, then unpacking them at the hotel.

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:58.297
And vivid is a virtual test camera, so you can test code without hardware.

00:02:59.497 --> 00:03:02.191
Here's the exact sequence from lab seven.

00:03:02.591 --> 00:03:07.583
The app checks capabilities, and sets the format: six forty by four eighty, YUYV.

00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:11.745
It requests four buffers, and maps each one into its memory.

00:03:12.145 --> 00:03:14.258
It queues all four, empty.

00:03:14.658 --> 00:03:15.981
It starts streaming.

00:03:16.381 --> 00:03:20.224
The hardware fills buffer zero by DMA and raises an interrupt.

00:03:20.624 --> 00:03:30.736
IRQ / interrupt: Interrupt request: a hardware signal that makes the processor stop and handle an event right now. Like: A doorbell: you stop what you are doing and answer it.

00:03:31.036 --> 00:03:32.894
The driver marks buffer zero done.

00:03:33.294 --> 00:03:38.135
The app's dequeue call returns buffer zero, full, with sequence number zero.

00:03:38.535 --> 00:03:44.201
The app uses it, for example encodes it, or sends it out over USB as a webcam.

00:03:44.601 --> 00:03:54.667
And queues it back. In our QEMU run with vivid, every frame came back with six hundred fourteen thousand four hundred bytes, exactly six forty times four eighty times two.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:04:03.380
QEMU: A program that emulates a whole computer, so you can test kernels without real hardware. Like: A flight simulator for software.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.614
Now the whole camera pipeline in a conferencing device.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.777
The image sensor produces raw Bayer data.

00:04:11.177 --> 00:04:14.788
It arrives over MIPI CSI two into the SoC's receiver.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:21.945
The image signal processor turns it into a real picture, and runs auto exposure, white balance and focus.

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:31.111
A scaler crops and zooms, for example for auto framing on the speaker. Buffers move between blocks as d m a buf file descriptors, with no copies.

00:04:31.511 --> 00:04:34.750
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.307
An encoder produces H two sixty four or M J P E G.

00:04:39.707 --> 00:04:45.570
H.264 / H.265: Standard video compression formats; H.265 needs about half the bits of H.264 for the same quality. Like: Two generations of vacuum-packing: the newer one squeezes tighter.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:53.475
YUY2 / NV12 / MJPEG: Ways to pack image pixels: brightness and colour stored separately (YUY2, NV12) or compressed per frame (MJPEG). Like: Different ways to pack a suitcase: rolled, folded, or vacuum-sealed.

00:04:53.725 --> 00:04:59.228
And the stream leaves either over USB as a webcam, or over the network as R T P.

00:05:00.428 --> 00:05:02.587
Conferencing lives or dies on audio.

00:05:02.987 --> 00:05:07.631
Echo cancellation needs the microphone and speaker on the same clock, or with known drift.

00:05:08.031 --> 00:05:11.572
Watch for over runs and under runs in the ALSA status files.

00:05:11.972 --> 00:05:18.300
The USB host's clock is different from your board's crystal, so use asynchronous U A C with a feedback endpoint.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:29.091
UAC / UAC2: USB Audio Class (version 2 is the modern one): the standard "I am a mic or speaker" language. Like: The same universal plug idea as the webcam language, but for sound.

00:05:29.391 --> 00:05:35.532
Endpoint: A numbered mailbox on a USB device; each data stream uses its own. Like: Numbered pigeonholes in an office: mail for video goes in one slot, audio in another.

00:05:35.782 --> 00:05:40.194
And for video, run v four l two compliance after every driver change.

00:05:41.394 --> 00:05:48.082
Question for you: Frames arrive but the picture is green. What is the first thing to compare between the sensor and the app?

00:05:53.382 --> 00:06:03.726
Answer: The pixel format and size each side agreed on. A mismatch, such as one packing format on one side and another on the other, gives wrong colors.

00:06:04.826 --> 00:06:05.523
Recap.

00:06:05.923 --> 00:06:09.069
Queue empty, dequeue full, use it, queue it again.

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:12.255
Hold buffers briefly, and allocate enough of them.

00:06:12.655 --> 00:06:15.651
Use d m a buf for zero copy between blocks.

00:06:16.051 --> 00:06:18.651
The media controller wires the pipeline.

00:06:19.051 --> 00:06:23.521
And for audio, keep one clock domain, or use asynchronous U A C.
